      "body": "> Can you reliably reproduce the race?\n\nSure, to repro the test issue:\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/src/validationinterface.cpp b/src/validationinterface.cpp\nindex 128f14a..06dd069 100644\n--- a/src/validationinterface.cpp\n+++ b/src/validationinterface.cpp\n@@ -17,2 +17,3 @@\n #include <util/task_runner.h>\n+#include <util/time.h>\n \n@@ -227,2 +228,7 @@ void ValidationSignals::BlockConnected(const ChainstateRole& role, std::shared_p\n     auto event = [role, pblock = std::move(pblock), pindex, this] {\n+        // TEST-ONLY RACE REPRODUCTION for wallet_assumeutxo.py. Keep the\n+        // height-357 callback queued while loadwallet(\"w\") registers its\n+        // validation handler. AttachChain() has a matching delay after the\n+        // handler is registered.\n+        if (pindex->nHeight == 357) UninterruptibleSleep(1s);\n         m_internals->Iterate([&](CValidationInterface& callbacks) { callbacks.BlockConnected(role, pblock, pindex); });\ndiff --git a/src/wallet/wallet.cpp b/src/wallet/wallet.cpp\nindex f509915..b2ca102 100644\n--- a/src/wallet/wallet.cpp\n+++ b/src/wallet/wallet.cpp\n@@ -3069,2 +3069,6 @@ bool CWallet::AttachChain(const std::shared_ptr<CWallet>& walletInstance, interf\n     walletInstance->m_chain_notifications_handler = walletInstance->chain().handleNotifications(walletInstance);\n+    // TEST-ONLY RACE REPRODUCTION for wallet_assumeutxo.py. cs_wallet remains\n+    // held here, so the queued height-357 callback retains this handler then\n+    // blocks trying to enter CWallet::blockConnected().\n+    UninterruptibleSleep(1s);\n \n```\n\nNot sure how often it would happen in reality during IBD catch-up. Maybe just the test can be fixed for now and then this issue can be closed or revisited later?\n\n<!--\n\n  A standalone setup would be:\n\n  1. Start one regtest node with pruning enabled.\n  2. Create victim, obtain an address, and unload it while its saved locator is still old.\n  3. Mine far enough ahead to permit pruning, then prune past victim’s locator.\n  4. Keep the victim address, and make a payment to it in a new tip block, H.\n  5. Arrange for BlockConnected(H) to remain queued.\n  6. Call loadwallet(\"victim\"). Its availability preflight fails because its rescan range crosses pruned data.\n  7. During that failed attach, dispatch the queued BlockConnected(H). It sees the newly registered victim, finds the payment, and persists its locator at H.\n  8. A later loadwallet(\"victim\") no longer needs the pruned range, so it succeeds but silently omits historic transactions.\n\n  The two test sleeps make steps 5–7 deterministic. The exact critical ordering is narrower than “a block arrives while wallet loading”:\n\n  queued BlockConnected(H)\n      ↓\n  AttachChain registers wallet, holding cs_wallet\n      ↓\n  validation thread enters Iterate() and retains the callback,\n  then blocks acquiring cs_wallet\n      ↓\n  availability preflight fails; AttachChain releases cs_wallet\n      ↓\n  callback acquires cs_wallet, updates a wallet tx, calls WriteBestBlock()\n      ↓\n  DisconnectChainNotifications() cannot cancel that already-in-flight callback\n\n  The validation iterator holds its internal mutex while invoking the callback, so teardown’s unregister blocks behind it; that makes the post-unlock handoff reliably favor the callback with the instrumentation.\n\n  It is naturally uncommon, but not exceptionally contrived:\n\n  • Failed load requires a pruned node, an assumeutxo background sync, or another missing-rescan-data condition.\n  • The relevant block must already be queued—not merely connected—when the handler is registered.\n  • That block must update the wallet (or be a periodic locator-write height).\n\n\n",
